    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-160013",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1fe5d8aa-8a79-5de4-8566-1432a640d78f",
      "knowledge_point": "Equating Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number to Solve For an Unknown Quantity",
      "problem": "Given that $\\frac{3}{4}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}=a+b\\text{i}$, calculate the value of $a$ and the value of $b.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we equate the real parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $b=\\sqrt{3}.$",
